#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222324} :activity/id #uuid "495fe43b-de66-5798-9669-4b55442f64d4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048881 :topic/id #uuid "c94f64e0-3901-584e-8de4-83ca8b66843b" :topic/math-academy-id 6378} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222494 :tutorial/content "Consider an autonomous system written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/60/60be83e58a72728b03ebfa2de6999ec80f17ee29e3f2ab3060d8ca43bbf45de8.svg) and ![](images/98/98bb67f212fa8deee63da1b69ae93086b9eb56a3ec64d70ba36e6a94dff1527b.svg).\n\nThe system is called **almost linear** at an equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ if the Jacobian determinant at this point is nonzero there, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\det\\!\\bigl(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\\bigr)\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, let\n![](images/e6/e640b7ca9ddc8252223ec70f011147a702cd25bca0ee8a20314693695c1bec40.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ so $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium.\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1+2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/93/93058061ca42daa7114b615bcb77a8f9ccbc9cdccec98559db3eb288a7208940.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/72/72feeca197610c24c70b01f4c1075c75eebe89ae29f1aced83caeb252c275cce.svg) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1+2(0) \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian is $0,$ the system is *not* almost linear at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$." :tutorial/id #uuid "fbbe8bd0-4cef-54d0-9803-a59eee727c6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14492 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6570665c-9c0e-5698-9c44-79ef60fe6401" :step/math-academy-id 591480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222325}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222325 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221182 :question/id #uuid "0f4ae74c-67d0-5c0e-99db-e834253b3851" :question/math-academy-id "e-21675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559846}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"} :step/id #uuid "4ea90440-2d8e-5c27-b532-f6be5d45b3b4" :step/math-academy-id 591481 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222326}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222496 :tutorial/content "Suppose the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nis almost linear at an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ In this case, the system's behavior near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ can be approximated by a linear system.\n\nFirst, we define the deviation from equilibrium by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nSmall values of $\\mathbf{u}$ correspond to points close to the equilibrium.\n\nNext, assuming $\\mathbf{x} = [x, y]^T,$ we compute the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/7a/7a0ff677dae0e51e34aed63faadfcb31f277f060c03488fdc1828cf8a2a5a0ec.svg)\nand evaluate it at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to obtain the constant matrix $J = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast).$\n\nFinally, since $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ the nonlinear system can be approximated by the **linearized system**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}'= J \\mathbf{u}.\n$$\n\nThis linear system captures the leading-order behavior of solutions close to the equilibrium.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9356e91f-f080-5827-a567-781aedac309a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14494 :tutorial/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "c10be260-d12b-595c-b241-523d377180f4" :step/math-academy-id 591484 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221418 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221181 :question/id #uuid "87b69a53-94f5-51e6-9c3a-e403abbf1fa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9aeac73-8ee3-5fef-a47c-a96e3545d89c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050780}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559818}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "264a8b35-ed46-5392-a8ec-e88ead83801a" :step/math-academy-id 591485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222495 :tutorial/content "Again, suppose $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium of the nonlinear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nand assume the system is *almost linear* at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. If \n\n$$\nJ=\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n$$\n\ndenotes the Jacobian matrix evaluated at the equilibrium, then the behavior of solutions near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is determined by the eigenvalues of $J.$ \n\nThis information can be summarized in the following table.\n\n| **Eigenvalues of the Jacobian** | **Behavior** | **Stability** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| real and both positive | source / unstable node | unstable |\n| real and both negative | sink / stable node | asymptotically stable |\n| real and opposite signs | saddle | unstable |\n| complex with positive real part | spiral source | unstable |\n| complex with negative real part | spiral sink | asymptotically stable |\n\nLet's now consider a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a8d4acde-0ca3-57a4-9994-a551a9d0094b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14493 :tutorial/title "Stability and Classification of Isolated Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "828feab6-cd8f-5d79-9356-a39ac111b298" :step/math-academy-id 591488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221419 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221183 :question/id #uuid "fd2d2d61-53a6-51c1-811c-41393270ddba" :question/math-academy-id "e-21676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "777fae40-2377-5e63-89f6-5c857beba2b2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050967}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559885}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying an Isolated Critical Point of a Nonlinear System"} :step/id #uuid "29c915bc-5c95-5594-b0cf-10edbe6f04a3" :step/math-academy-id 591489 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222497 :tutorial/content "In the classification above, we intentionally skipped one case: \n\nThe eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ of the Jacobian at the equilibrium are purely imaginary. \n\nIn this situation, the corresponding linearized system has a *center* at the origin, meaning that solutions neither grow nor decay, but instead circulate around the equilibrium.\n\nFor the original nonlinear system, however, this case is *inconclusive*. While the linearized system predicts closed orbits, the nonlinear system *may* exhibit a stable center, an unstable spiral, or other nearby behavior.\n\nTherefore, when the Jacobian has purely imaginary eigenvalues, linearization alone is not sufficient to classify the equilibrium. In such cases, further analysis beyond linear approximation is required." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d168b25-7771-5e55-8be8-2eb16080b9b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14496 :tutorial/title "A Note About Stable Centers"} :step/id #uuid "d039d8e2-81e7-5160-bcfd-7765a694f63a" :step/math-academy-id 591492}] :activity/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221470}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510488} :activity/id #uuid "c7291f65-7a36-5e3f-9242-7648b94cd8a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048881 :topic/id #uuid "c94f64e0-3901-584e-8de4-83ca8b66843b" :topic/math-academy-id 6378} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a4fe5c5-df45-542c-8137-a773f298d44c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368145} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ca71f75-d635-5941-822d-a4497e7aa8a8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9275c133-5427-5250-a5e9-cd464196f7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368147} :answer-field/id #uuid "462a7a1d-db14-542b-ab8e-76df5f3ae7c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368146} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e00afc3b-0f8c-511a-ae3d-a6b6cdc86dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368149} :answer-field/id #uuid "832fa3f0-8f05-504a-b52e-696bd0e5e7a1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4643b74-0553-58af-a597-f560a8bf34f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368151} :answer-field/id #uuid "24980e91-6228-5dad-a305-d83da3126fce" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "52a5f397-6ab6-5b30-a51f-fa3f7be9877d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335130" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2y \\\\ -3x+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3x+{y}^{2})=-3,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(0) & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2cb4bb92-6182-5d82-adf8-726f3c1f7eaa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34ade299-a613-5a23-987e-23a091542225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509800} {:answer/id #uuid "866d6d61-ec02-5461-a9af-e13501b74bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509980} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d35f3f-1137-54b9-bd30-758c448db7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509980} :answer-field/id #uuid "b50e47a7-1678-588a-bc35-ad270c59097b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1ef6b60-5a30-5b50-bc9b-fd1b1fc59d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510097} {:answer/id #uuid "f054de3f-c85f-5fce-886d-2896608021dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510204} :answer-field/id #uuid "c715942c-d2c2-5b4c-ae37-5167cb54c7d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "83d63d6d-d88e-5a5d-adf3-8972165ae385" :question/math-academy-id "q-336954" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=xy-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system’s solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}={[2,\\text{ }1]}^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an  \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}xy-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{y}^{2}-1)=y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{2}-1)=x-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5)=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & x-2y \\\\ -2x & -2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}(1) & 2-2(1) \\\\ -2(2) & -2(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2,1.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"} :step/id #uuid "372bc3a9-4bde-54ec-8f9c-e9a568ca29f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5a55fdf-f65f-595c-83d0-6e0cb10b7741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510104} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fb562b6-3ffd-5817-bd9b-9192877cbc82" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510271} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8397406b-3741-58c6-be48-cb822d70a432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509977} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1006ef8-7090-5491-a5a2-d9fd36d5275f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510321} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "216ca193-a489-5830-9a58-4e49ef86790a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509756} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b52cf4-5cc3-561a-bfeb-9f02941ae255" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd4ad32-eeac-5002-877f-b890f2b10896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509912} :answer-field/id #uuid "f24f971e-0879-530e-bc3b-f3a08731a8aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59916f2b-317a-58b9-929b-11913732d3cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335133" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-5y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+{y}^{2})=4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-5y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-5y)=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2y \\\\ 2x & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "70dd2bb3-126f-59ed-855a-f46120b28c5e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed2047a8-8982-5263-b25f-46e01db0123d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368177} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a3a8240-1762-543d-99d2-3372e89367f7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2da43c9-f82c-5c88-b1e6-691df9d8c9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368179} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d3bca15-6c24-52f3-baa6-76c81a9a965b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57371dc2-00f9-5847-bcf0-bf21a9eee108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368181} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8f71a2-83e7-5924-ab0b-12a5484830f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e2d6566-7c2c-5700-9daf-b1f1f46b1e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368183} :answer-field/id #uuid "3396f4c5-a888-5322-87d4-018f368e9efa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "396d0927-5f69-505a-80d1-a119800f214e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336900" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-3y(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2y-6 \\\\ x-3y+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=-3+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2y-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(2) & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)-3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "819b91a6-5d0d-5137-8403-162591cc718d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ea5697-e4ef-54af-b81f-7e25b01bce9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368203} {:answer/id #uuid "9810ecd9-fc7e-5f2f-a0da-32c49e519e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368203} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee66c07-aedb-5dc2-9782-17c5870e5ecd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368202} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc40fa7-8e1e-5f9b-ad4e-31114d9b1e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368206} {:answer/id #uuid "70f5be1c-7ce8-5c70-9672-5f4d7be10919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368207} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6e4ec8-2ac9-5ab9-907d-334bae85047c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368207} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a03ba21-b40c-5305-8798-d44f1ddced2f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ce4d3c5-f9b2-510f-a42c-6aedb52f013f" :question/math-academy-id "q-337695" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t)+x(t)^{3} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+y(t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y+{x}^{3} \\\\ -2x+{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =2+3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3{x}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2+0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$"} :step/id #uuid "ecc8f232-f4d0-52b1-8514-442214380c29"}] :activity/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509690}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046