edb/ref 17592186559694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b3908e50-0fb9-59ae-a2c0-f103ab5a2672" :question/math-academy-id "q-335101" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x(t))+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0)(0) & {0}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"} :step/id #uuid "501033d8-e454-5811-b966-77d481fbff5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17579c5b-eadf-528d-baf5-2c0423246f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368136} :answer-field/id #uuid "572486b8-bc89-5344-92c2-8aa2c6647272" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159a880b-1a99-5587-abb7-68aa96e623ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368138} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d635799-f303-5522-909a-2afd205ed96a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368137} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed430409-83a9-5b2f-a38b-4e4cbf815f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368140} :answer-field/id #uuid "53bcde91-822d-54e8-93d6-e29c65078012" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368139} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d4041d2-5655-5f55-b703-8da986fba1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368142} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce4a0d20-92b4-5bba-907e-7a5e5a3873ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4cf8bbe-a8bb-5848-ae43-4a663ad26c71" :question/math-academy-id "q-335128" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={y}^{2}(t)-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-x \\\\ {x}^{2}+2y\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-x)=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-x)=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2y \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "593a3c9e-9d69-56ea-ae20-d8b446ff32c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7bc9df-4eca-55e0-9b4f-1a80b2401b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559160} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eeedb10-572f-5dd3-bedc-0b1891a82abe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b788f6-009e-557e-8638-cb1bada0e9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559113} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d5c020c-d812-5830-bb16-2fde66145db3" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "456d796f-9080-5d24-aff1-a5093a7534f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559198} :answer-field/id #uuid "e491bcb7-c400-552a-bcdc-d27ce3349eb6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "963a67aa-962f-5d59-9155-0724e9cbe8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559392}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559392} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc4e3ec0-7449-5311-a9a7-e21685e5a48c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7b1e877f-fd37-5577-a12f-56b4c019e4b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-336907" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-2x(t)-3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4y-2x-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4y-2x-3)=4 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3y-3)=-2y+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & 4 \\\\ 2x & -2y+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1)-2 & 4 \\\\ 2(1) & -2(1)+3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "987ac928-c043-5745-b107-d0a7b5b8d27a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559961}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559961 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55acf14a-7200-5cda-93be-9d5945787124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559235} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd8a3e1-68b3-519c-853a-2545ec83e1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559326} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d37d7a9-8471-50da-b073-2955e9e0599c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559731} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228bfc62-fb62-5294-a07c-a1a29f6ba804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559099} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae6b8de-662b-54bb-a0ce-647e75cdc4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559212} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a1e0d7-eefa-5285-8c61-39a8788320e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559099} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6eb5f28-ac4d-5b95-b64b-baa3dc1708f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f52e822f-6421-5efd-a62a-efb0d9241bfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-336950" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}+xy-6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}+2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}={[2,\\text{ }1]}^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy-6 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+2x-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy-6)=x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+2x-3)=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x \\\\ 2 & -2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(2)+1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{2}.$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are real and have opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b1d288d6-ba48-510c-9a5c-e09c1e217944"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5748e2c-7fb4-5637-a5a3-98008420dcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220458} {:answer/id #uuid "e26e4f33-951a-52a6-8f03-50c2309df66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220459} {:answer/id #uuid "3524e7ad-dbe7-5691-be30-938fdcd8d02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220460}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ce6c0f42-70c6-5c07-8f96-8128176aff08" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbc676ee-3532-5bd0-9486-69e88dd2701e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220462} {:answer/id #uuid "0693bec2-98c7-54ea-a812-3416e3c833cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220463}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ead8c9e6-97c1-5e31-9dc8-073af10fcbec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220461} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9244b400-42ab-58df-b8b0-a3bbc7cba75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391213} {:answer/id #uuid "0daed90d-a153-582e-90da-175afd8fd964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391214} {:answer/id #uuid "602df48b-efc0-58a3-a4e8-f8eafc30ceaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391213} :answer-field/id #uuid "2afcd599-57e3-5297-b831-71b01faf8aa5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391212} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2c67e4d-8b9b-5f92-a771-1827d4638f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391217} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd6bf2a-d1b6-56e4-984f-6951a8cdadff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391218} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4eec63b-b845-54c1-bfe7-24f2abb290f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e06a687f-dc13-5f88-9f73-09d6b8609d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-333532" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x(t))+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=1-{x}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 1 \\\\ -2(1) & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda \\cdot (-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c347bf5d-5480-57e0-a437-6e24c7d9755b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559927}}] :activity/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559009}]}
